"Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=10$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338533/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "8802af8b-4dde-5a58-a51e-084a51ffb842" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338533/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2abac178-ade9-5f83-9fe1-6252892ec145" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338533/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "40c24394-e973-59d1-9167-634cf777f21e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338533/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "13231aec-e1bb-5d83-b2ea-584488714b61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338533/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "a3d74180-5d78-5e19-a40e-3203ce946229" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338533/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a9729b-f977-5b73-a7a7-48f5c43c8f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-338533/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-338533/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-338533/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-338533/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-338533/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-338533/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338533" :question/id #uuid "ec6d918c-1e89-56ba-9337-5b4cab88266e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338533" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,y(5)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(-3)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-3)=1$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338533/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338533"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131001/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "628c02e9-e153-5d65-8232-75b1538b6bc7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131001/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1d3f2c93-6a05-50d5-bb43-35203c048ef0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131001/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "7005190e-e172-58bd-986b-51724efb5d90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131001/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ec4604f3-de94-5e52-b7d6-ef5649b76ca7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131001/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "fb98d297-b305-5b1f-aabb-31785113c708" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131001/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f97b2fa5-35c7-528d-8346-6234742f1220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131001/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-131001/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-131001/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-131001/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-131001/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-131001/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131001" :question/id #uuid "5d885e48-2e79-58d6-9707-a6c9e1d542f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-131001" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=1,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=x{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131001/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131001"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6566"] :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n\n- $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n\n- $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny''+y'=x \\cos x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0,$ and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nyy'' - y'+ 9 = 0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6565"] :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n- $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n\n- $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n\n- $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6568"] :question/problem "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6567"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n\n- $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1,  \\quad  y'(4)= 1$\n\n- $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0,  \\quad  y'(1)= 5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
